#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Project-local Jev arbiter: short-lived CLI, no service or socket.

Hook: JSON stdin -> JSON stdout; the isolated Codex profile invokes it.
Setup another project: python3 judge.py init /absolute/project/path
Artifacts: .toolreview/turns/SESSION/TURN/{events,recipe_versions,approvals}/
One active turn recipe: turns/SESSION/TURN/recipe.json (whole-file replacements).
Review learned layers: python3 judge.py candidates
Prepare an INACTIVE inventory: python3 judge.py consolidate --scope project --target /project
Append a human-authored restriction: python3 judge.py promote recipe.json

Approved recipes are ordered by filename. Scope mismatches and confident semantic
non-matches skip only that recipe. Unknown applicability escalates, never skips.
Permanent generalized allow recipes are unsupported. V3 learned layers only
support base-cleared actions in their originating turn and launch directory.

This is a cooperative guardrail, not an unmodifiable security boundary. The
project owner/agent can edit files. Codex's sandbox/normal approvals still apply;
native hook failures/timeouts can fail open, and write_stdin/hosted tools are not
fully intercepted. Jev confidence and LLM self-rated confidence are not proofs.
The fallback is a separate request to the configured resolver model. It may
share weights with the working agent; independence is not guaranteed. Credentials come from the environment or a configured command, not project files.
"""
